\section{NA handling — the key difference}{

For \code{w = "FLQuants"}: \code{NA} values in \code{x} are zeroed out for the
numerator, and the corresponding weight is also zeroed via an internal
NA-flag \code{FLQuants}, so an \code{NA} element contributes neither to the
weighted sum nor to the sum of weights at that position. A small constant
(\code{1e-36}) is added to the summed weights to avoid division by zero where
every weight is zero. If the result is \emph{still} \code{NA} at some position
(e.g. every element of \code{x} was \code{NA} there), it is replaced by the plain,
unweighted arithmetic mean of \code{x}, \code{Reduce('+', x) / length(x)} — note
this fallback is computed from the original \code{x}, so it can itself remain
\code{NA} if \code{x} is \code{NA} at every element for that position; this method has
no \code{na.rm} argument, the behaviour above is not toggleable.

For \code{w = "numeric"}: NA handling is controlled by \code{na.rm}. With the
default \code{na.rm = TRUE}, \code{NA} values are excluded from both the weighted
sum and the sum of weights (\code{wsum}, tracked explicitly rather than via an
epsilon), so a missing index does not dilute the indices that do have
data; positions where every element of \code{x} is \code{NA} return \code{NA} directly,
with no arithmetic-mean fallback. With \code{na.rm = FALSE}, no special
handling is applied and any \code{NA} in \code{x} propagates to the result, as for
the default \code{stats::weighted.mean}.
